02:31.249 --> 02:31.749
All right.

02:31.859 --> 02:33.259
Let's talk about your morning.

02:33.539 --> 02:34.939
See if this sounds familiar.

02:35.729 --> 02:38.919
You don't set up an alarm, or
maybe you do and ignore it.

02:39.249 --> 02:41.129
You wake up at different time every day.

02:41.339 --> 02:45.379
Monday at nine, Wednesday at eleven,
Friday, you don't even check.

02:46.269 --> 02:50.119
You slept seven hours, maybe eight,
and you feel like you slept two.

02:50.369 --> 02:54.049
Your body got the hours, but
somehow it didn't get the rest.

02:54.699 --> 02:56.449
You figured you'd shake it off by noon.

02:56.929 --> 02:57.609
You didn't.

02:57.769 --> 02:59.739
Two PM and brain fog hits.

03:00.019 --> 03:03.939
You read the same Slack message three
times and still don't know what it says.

03:04.439 --> 03:07.729
You switch to easy tasks,
anything to avoid real thinking.

03:08.089 --> 03:10.859
A third cup of coffee,
it's not working anymore.

03:11.079 --> 03:15.059
You hit a wall, and you know the rest
of the day is going to be a struggle.

03:15.569 --> 03:18.269
Then eleven PM, you're exhausted.

03:18.459 --> 03:19.549
Your body is done.

03:20.049 --> 03:22.059
But your brain, it won't shut off.

03:22.119 --> 03:26.999
You lie there, staring at the ceiling,
and then three AM, wide awake, not because

03:26.999 --> 03:28.929
of a noise, not because of a nightmare.

03:29.119 --> 03:30.929
Your brain just won't stop.

03:31.279 --> 03:32.919
If this is you, you're not alone.

03:32.999 --> 03:38.009
I went through the exact same thing, and
the data backs it up because over forty

03:38.009 --> 03:41.969
percent of people who work from home
wake up repeatedly during the night.

03:42.299 --> 03:45.729
It's a sign that pattern has gone
deep, and by morning you feel

03:45.729 --> 03:47.299
worse than when you went to bed.

03:48.129 --> 03:50.819
And the worst part, you stop noticing.

03:51.079 --> 03:52.689
Being tired became your normal.

03:52.869 --> 03:54.699
You don't say, "I'm exhausted," anymore.

03:54.749 --> 03:58.529
You say, "I'm fine. Same as
always." But you're not fine.

03:58.539 --> 04:03.029
You're running at sixty percent
every single day, and you don't

04:03.029 --> 04:06.449
even realize it because you forgot
what a hundred percent feels like.

04:07.469 --> 04:07.789
All right.

04:07.799 --> 04:09.099
Think about your workday.

04:09.589 --> 04:11.469
You work eight, 10 hours.

04:11.709 --> 04:14.989
Let's be honest, when you work
from home, it's rarely just eight.

04:15.219 --> 04:17.499
But how many of those are actually sharp?

04:17.819 --> 04:19.959
Maybe three, four, if you're lucky.

04:20.129 --> 04:24.129
The rest, you're just, just there at
your desk, going through the motions.

04:24.659 --> 04:28.609
But here's what most people miss: it's not
because you lack discipline.

04:28.949 --> 04:32.559
Your prefrontal cortex, the part
of your brain that handles focus,

04:32.559 --> 04:36.929
judgment, planning, impulse control,
is the first thing that shuts down

04:36.929 --> 04:38.829
when your brain hasn't fully recovered.

04:39.449 --> 04:41.149
That's not a character flaw.

04:41.379 --> 04:45.729
It's like driving a car with the engine
light on and the oil almost empty.

04:45.989 --> 04:49.149
The car still moves, but
it's slow, it overheats, and

04:49.179 --> 04:50.879
every mile does more damage.

04:51.259 --> 04:52.619
That's your brain right now.

04:52.899 --> 04:57.829
It still works, but everything costs twice
the effort and gives you half the result.

04:58.459 --> 05:01.619
And every decision you make
in that state, it costs you

05:01.619 --> 05:03.939
in time, in quality, in money.

05:04.589 --> 05:10.019
And what nobody talks about, you
can't fix this by working more hours.

05:10.499 --> 05:14.379
Working ten hours instead of eight
doesn't make you more productive.

05:14.589 --> 05:16.389
It makes you more exhausted tomorrow.

05:17.119 --> 05:19.929
You're not underperforming
because you're not working enough.

05:20.369 --> 05:24.209
You are underperforming because your brain
can't use the hours you already have.

05:24.549 --> 05:28.299
The problem isn't quantity of
hours, it's the quality of what

05:28.309 --> 05:30.109
your brain does with those hours.

05:30.559 --> 05:33.249
And that quality is determined
by something most people

05:33.249 --> 05:36.127
completely ignore. All right.

05:36.737 --> 05:40.107
Think about the last time
you had a drink or two.

05:40.227 --> 05:42.567
Not drunk, just two glasses of wine.

05:43.077 --> 05:44.147
Remember that state?

05:44.507 --> 05:46.547
A little impulsive, a little scattered.

05:47.087 --> 05:51.167
You misjudge things, situations,
people, your own decisions.

05:51.747 --> 05:53.527
You wouldn't sign a contract like that.

05:53.897 --> 05:55.917
You wouldn't make a
business call, would you?

05:56.827 --> 05:58.647
Now, here's the uncomfortable part.

05:58.977 --> 06:03.577
When your brain runs on incomplete
recovery week after week, it

06:03.577 --> 06:05.157
settles into the same range.

06:05.637 --> 06:09.037
The researchers have measured people in
exactly that state:

06:09.977 --> 06:13.597
reaction time, judgment, focus,
performing like they'd been drinking.

06:13.877 --> 06:17.067
And the people themselves,
they swore they were fine.

06:17.657 --> 06:20.007
The longer it went on,
the less they notice.

06:20.427 --> 06:22.967
You're not making decisions
with your full brain.

06:23.127 --> 06:27.897
You're making them a little drunk every
single day, and you don't feel it.

06:28.587 --> 06:31.677
You probably told
yourself this is burnout.

06:31.807 --> 06:34.927
Too much work, too much
stress, and you need a break.

06:35.387 --> 06:39.427
So you take one, maybe a long weekend,
maybe a week off, maybe longer.

06:39.917 --> 06:45.497
I took a year off, a full year of
doing nothing, and nothing changed.

06:45.647 --> 06:47.837
I rested and still felt destroyed.

06:48.347 --> 06:50.567
And here's what I didn't
understand at the time.

06:50.807 --> 06:54.297
I thought if I just removed the
load, the pressure, the deadlines,

06:54.707 --> 06:56.607
the work, I would recover.

06:57.457 --> 06:58.827
That's what everyone thinks.

06:59.007 --> 07:02.107
But removing the load and
actually recovering are two

07:02.107 --> 07:03.477
completely different things.

07:03.777 --> 07:05.707
It's like tearing a muscle.

07:05.917 --> 07:09.057
You don't fix a torn muscle
by lying on the couch longer.

07:09.697 --> 07:14.327
A torn muscle needs targeted
rehab, specific exercises in the

07:14.327 --> 07:16.007
right order at the right time.

07:16.237 --> 07:18.397
Without that, it just heals wrong.

07:18.937 --> 07:21.137
You need a completely
different kind of fix.

07:21.707 --> 07:26.387
A vacation is a wrong tool for the job
because burnout is the label we put on it.

07:26.677 --> 07:27.757
It's not the cause.

07:28.047 --> 07:30.827
The cause is something that
doesn't fix itself with rest.

07:31.267 --> 07:34.927
If time off actually fixed
this, you'd already be fine.

07:35.307 --> 07:36.907
Think about your last vacation.

07:37.117 --> 07:40.387
Did you come back sharp, or did
the fog come back within days?

07:41.747 --> 07:44.177
And here's the thing
that nobody tells you.

07:44.417 --> 07:46.537
This isn't a bad week or a bad month.

07:46.727 --> 07:49.237
It's a cycle, and it's getting stronger.

07:49.787 --> 07:51.947
I'm talking about exhaustion cycle.

07:52.877 --> 07:54.787
Low energy leads to more caffeine.

07:55.047 --> 07:57.257
More caffeine leads to afternoon crash.

07:57.627 --> 08:00.527
And now everyone dips
between two and four PM.

08:00.537 --> 08:02.527
That's just how your body is built.

08:02.677 --> 08:04.577
It's basic physiology.

08:04.587 --> 08:05.627
Everyone has it.

08:06.157 --> 08:09.787
But when you're already on, running
on empty, the dip becomes a crash

08:09.787 --> 08:13.757
you can't work through, and in
that state, even naps don't work.

08:13.767 --> 08:15.097
You're still wired at night.

08:15.347 --> 08:16.427
Can't switch off.

08:16.637 --> 08:18.837
Wake up drained, and the cycle repeats.

08:19.177 --> 08:20.457
Every day a little worse.

08:20.487 --> 08:23.097
Every day, uh, every week
a little harder to break.

08:24.619 --> 08:26.459
All right, let's talk about caffeine.

08:26.759 --> 08:31.129
After a few weeks of daily coffee,
caffeine almost stops giving you energy.

08:31.129 --> 08:33.319
It just prevents withdrawal symptoms.

08:33.689 --> 08:35.899
Your morning cup, it doesn't boost you.

08:36.079 --> 08:37.539
It brings you back to zero.

08:37.999 --> 08:39.249
You're not getting a lift.

08:39.249 --> 08:42.489
You're just digging out of
hole that caffeine created,

08:42.849 --> 08:44.759
and the cycle builds tolerance.

08:44.919 --> 08:49.759
You need more to feel the same until
even three cups barely moves the needle.

08:50.769 --> 08:53.789
All right, if any of this
sounds familiar, stay with me.

08:53.799 --> 08:57.759
The next part is where it starts to
make sense because I went through

08:58.009 --> 09:03.619
all of this myself, and it took me three
years to find out the way. Okay.

09:04.229 --> 09:07.869
Let me tell you my story, not
because it's special, but because

09:07.949 --> 09:09.719
it's probably a lot like yours.

09:10.429 --> 09:15.839
During the pandemic, I had to find a
remote job, and at first I was happy.

09:15.979 --> 09:19.749
I could work from home, from my
room, sometimes even from my bed.

09:20.169 --> 09:23.539
Life was good, but the schedule was tough.

09:24.049 --> 09:27.869
Work ended at eleven PM, sometimes midnight,
sometimes one AM.

09:28.299 --> 09:29.969
There was no fixed end time.

09:30.359 --> 09:31.939
I had no time to recover.

09:32.069 --> 09:33.489
My schedule fell apart.

09:33.759 --> 09:36.849
One night I'd crash at
one AM, the next at three.

09:37.429 --> 09:39.669
Every morning I woke up feeling destroyed.

09:40.079 --> 09:45.469
Everything turned i-into one loop:
work, late-night TV and phone, midnight

09:45.469 --> 09:47.559
snacks that were really full meals.

09:47.959 --> 09:51.799
And over time, I got angry
and snappy from being tired.

09:52.629 --> 09:55.449
By evening, I was too drained
to do anything good with my day.

09:55.559 --> 09:57.749
I was drinking coffee nonstop.

09:58.089 --> 09:59.969
My cup was like a bottomless pit.

10:00.479 --> 10:03.409
No energy for exercise,
no energy for real food.

10:03.919 --> 10:05.519
I ate whatever I could find.

10:05.779 --> 10:09.689
Every day I burned out a little
more, and stress kept building.

10:10.219 --> 10:14.529
I'd scrape together my last bit of
energy to finish a task or get on a call.

10:14.729 --> 10:16.009
I had to be online.

10:16.519 --> 10:18.859
But to be fair, the company was great.

10:19.089 --> 10:22.109
Good people, good culture,
supported every step.

10:22.439 --> 10:26.789
But that wasn't enough because
only I could fix what I had broken.

10:27.229 --> 10:31.189
At some point, I burned out
completely, and that's how my career

10:31.189 --> 10:33.109
ended after almost three years.

10:34.129 --> 10:36.199
And I thought I'd restart.

10:36.419 --> 10:38.039
Jumped into crypto investing.

10:38.509 --> 10:42.009
Loved the freedom, worked
whenever I want, no nine-to-five.

10:42.309 --> 10:44.719
You know, fell right into the same hole.

10:45.319 --> 10:49.129
I'd wake up at night to check
positions, one bad call after

10:49.129 --> 10:54.169
another, so I lost seventy thousand
dollars, my entire portfolio,

10:54.169 --> 10:56.819
plus part of our family f-savings.

10:57.839 --> 10:58.749
That was tough.

10:59.649 --> 11:03.819
So I took a break and then finally
decided to build a business I'd

11:03.819 --> 11:08.059
been thinking about for years, the
idea I kept putting off, you know?

11:08.749 --> 11:12.619
I took a 5K business course from Alex
Becker, hanging in a private Facebook

11:12.649 --> 11:15.309
group all the time before he shut it down.

11:15.499 --> 11:19.529
Studied hard, did everything
right, but I had no energy.

11:19.739 --> 11:23.189
Brain fog every day and
cut corners everywhere.

11:23.579 --> 11:26.959
It all fell apart. Third time in a row, like
stepping on

11:26.959 --> 11:28.969
the same rake again and again.

11:30.027 --> 11:33.397
I kept ignoring the one
thing that mattered, my body.

11:33.717 --> 11:39.077
So I decided to just stop, take a
break, stop everything and rest.

11:39.407 --> 11:40.777
But rest didn't help.

11:41.087 --> 11:43.487
One month turn-turned to-- into six.

11:43.867 --> 11:45.437
Six turned into a year.

11:45.727 --> 11:47.697
A full year of doing nothing.

11:47.707 --> 11:49.747
I had no energy for friends.

11:49.767 --> 11:52.327
Even texting back felt like a big deal.

11:52.897 --> 11:54.267
No energy for my wife.

11:54.717 --> 11:57.027
Communication felt like an obligation.

11:57.197 --> 12:00.647
I couldn't even play video
games, and I love video games.

12:01.077 --> 12:06.987
All I did was sleep, eat, drift around the
house, watch TV, then sleep again.

12:07.337 --> 12:11.157
I'd nap twice a day, and the
only time I felt some-somewhat

12:11.157 --> 12:13.007
alive was after midnight.

12:13.587 --> 12:17.777
I would stay up until two, three,
four AM, and the cycle repeated.

12:18.377 --> 12:22.997
But then my mom came to visit, and
moms don't let you drift, you know?

12:23.477 --> 12:26.897
We walked a lot, beach,
sun, food at normal hours.

12:26.927 --> 12:28.577
No time for afternoon naps.

12:28.907 --> 12:32.627
By evening, I was exhausted,
but a different kind of

12:32.627 --> 12:34.397
exhausted, the good kind.

12:34.867 --> 12:39.797
I started falling asleep at ten, 11
PM, and within days, something shifted.

12:40.217 --> 12:41.137
I lost weight.

12:41.347 --> 12:42.467
The snoring stopped.

12:42.787 --> 12:46.657
I started going outside on my
own, talking to my wife because I

12:46.667 --> 12:48.927
wanted to, not because I had to.

12:49.317 --> 12:51.927
But here's the part
that actually mattered.

12:52.397 --> 12:55.487
Somewhere in that second week, it hit me.

12:55.777 --> 12:56.797
I remember this.

12:56.957 --> 13:01.827
This is what it feels like to
want things again, to have energy

13:01.837 --> 13:04.177
and something to spend it on.

13:04.537 --> 13:08.237
I'd been foggy for so long,
I forgot this state existed.

13:08.797 --> 13:10.997
Turns out, the old me wasn't gone.

13:11.357 --> 13:16.087
He was still there the whole
time, but I had no idea what had

13:16.087 --> 13:17.877
switched him back on, you know?

13:18.537 --> 13:19.997
Then my mom left.

13:20.587 --> 13:25.387
For a week or two, I held the rhythm,
and then the late nights crept back, the

13:25.387 --> 13:28.087
naps came back, and the fog came back.

13:28.727 --> 13:33.277
And this time, it scared me because
now I knew exactly what I was losing.

13:33.687 --> 13:34.677
I'd tasted it.

13:35.177 --> 13:40.997
I was watching the clear version of me
dissolve in real time, and I couldn't

13:40.997 --> 13:44.767
stop it because I didn't know what
had created him in the first place.

13:45.537 --> 13:50.017
If you ever come back from a vacation
feeling sharp and lost it within a week,

13:50.257 --> 13:58.051
you know this exact feeling. Or those random
sharp days that show up out of nowhere,

13:58.401 --> 14:02.341
when the work just flows and gone by
Thursday.

14:02.721 --> 14:04.381
A lottery you don't control.

14:04.861 --> 14:09.581
Whatever happened during those weeks, it
wasn't willpower, it wasn't motivation.

14:09.781 --> 14:13.121
Something had reset in my
body, and without knowing what

14:13.121 --> 14:14.641
it was, I couldn't keep it.

14:15.401 --> 14:17.321
That fear is what started everything.

14:17.821 --> 14:18.961
I wasn't curious.

14:18.961 --> 14:20.801
I was running from that hole.

14:21.551 --> 14:27.591
So I started going on evening walks, the
one habit I'd managed to keep, and kept

14:27.601 --> 14:31.661
digging: health podcasts, performance
optimization, hundreds of results.

14:32.141 --> 14:35.681
And at some point, I just got
tired, tired of the noise.

14:36.071 --> 14:38.961
Every guru selling a morning
routine, every influencer

14:38.961 --> 14:40.721
pushing another supplement stack.

14:41.261 --> 14:44.451
Empty information recycled over and over.

14:44.661 --> 14:46.721
And I didn't want another hack.

14:46.831 --> 14:49.071
I wanted a signal in all that noise.

14:49.591 --> 14:53.771
And then scrolling through
podcasts, I saw an episode with

14:53.771 --> 14:55.781
a doctor, an actual doctor.

14:56.761 --> 15:00.501
The title was something like,
"How to get your clarity or--

15:00.651 --> 15:02.681
and energy back" or something.

15:03.091 --> 15:03.931
And I thought,

15:06.365 --> 15:09.395
What if this was never
a productivity question?

15:09.875 --> 15:14.405
What if this is a medical question, and
real evidence-based medicine has been

15:14.415 --> 15:16.535
studying this state the whole time?

15:16.935 --> 15:21.695
Maybe it was time to stop listening to
bloggers and find out what actual doctors

15:21.705 --> 15:24.655
say about this and how you get out of it.

15:25.525 --> 15:31.945
And next to this doctor was a word,
uh, I'd never seen in my life,

15:32.115 --> 15:37.495
somnologist, a doctor whose entire specialty
is why the brain stops

15:37.505 --> 15:39.975
performing and what actually fixes it.

15:40.405 --> 15:44.515
I pressed play, and by the end of that
evening walk, for the first time in three

15:44.515 --> 15:46.875
years, the picture started making sense.

15:48.295 --> 15:50.965
All right, let me tell
you who this man is.

15:51.265 --> 15:55.875
A clinical somnologist, over thirty years
of practice, the kind of doctor CEOs

15:55.875 --> 16:00.225
and executives fly in to see, people
who can't afford to think at half speed.

16:00.775 --> 16:04.025
His first consultation costs
over six hundred dollars.

16:04.445 --> 16:06.825
And here's the part that
changed everything for me.

16:07.045 --> 16:07.965
I went deeper.

16:08.305 --> 16:11.055
The books, the lectures,
the whole clinical system.

16:11.545 --> 16:14.035
Almost none of it had been
translated to English.

16:14.275 --> 16:17.545
That's why you've probably
never heard of any of this.

16:18.115 --> 16:19.045
You didn't miss it.

16:19.285 --> 16:21.165
It was never there to find.

16:21.945 --> 16:24.965
Putting it all together into
one system took months of work.

16:25.595 --> 16:29.515
Not generic tips, actual clinical
protocols tested on thousands

16:29.515 --> 16:31.395
of patients over decades.

16:31.765 --> 16:34.595
What to do, what not to
do, and in what order.

16:35.195 --> 16:38.005
And for the first time,
I was, I wasn't guessing.

16:38.325 --> 16:43.205
I understood what was broken and why it broke
and how to fix it.

16:43.625 --> 16:45.255
I remember the exact feeling.

16:45.275 --> 16:48.625
I'm holding something
nobody around me has.

16:49.385 --> 16:51.035
So I started applying it.

16:51.235 --> 16:52.765
Not random tips this time.

16:52.945 --> 16:55.255
I did it the way the protocol said.

16:55.695 --> 16:58.855
Step by step, each piece
on top of the last.

16:59.285 --> 17:01.185
And I felt myself coming back.

17:01.405 --> 17:05.515
Clarity first, fewer bad
thoughts, less static in my head.

17:06.035 --> 17:11.315
Then energy, the part I'd missed the most,
wanting to do things again, like when

17:11.315 --> 17:13.435
I was younger and had energy to burn.

17:14.135 --> 17:17.125
And here's the difference
from those weeks with my mom.

17:17.425 --> 17:22.405
That version of me had shown
up, had shown by accident and

17:22.435 --> 17:24.655
dissolved the moment life got busy.

17:25.045 --> 17:28.985
This time, I knew exactly
what was creating him.

17:29.345 --> 17:31.065
So this time, he stayed.

17:31.435 --> 17:32.225
He's still here.

17:32.225 --> 17:34.445
He's the one talking to you right now.

17:35.035 --> 17:38.125
And back then, I called my friend
and couldn't shut up about it.

17:38.635 --> 17:42.375
And he started making changes too,
not because I pushed him, because

17:42.455 --> 17:44.635
he saw what was happening to me.

17:44.885 --> 17:46.565
And that's when it hit me.

17:46.765 --> 17:51.405
Everyone was walking around broken, and
nobody told them it can be fixed.

17:52.223 --> 17:55.173
I felt like I took NZT
from the movie Limitless.

17:55.593 --> 17:58.103
I could see what to do with
my life and how to do it.

17:58.833 --> 18:03.253
I wake up, I have my morning routine,
and then just go, task after task.

18:03.643 --> 18:06.063
When stress hits, I act, I don’t spiral.

18:06.193 --> 18:11.263
Solutions that used to be hidden behind a
wall of brain fog, they’re just there now.

18:11.993 --> 18:16.313
Things I’d been putting off for months
started getting done in hours, not

18:16.423 --> 18:20.653
because I suddenly had h-- superhuman
discipline, because my brain was

18:20.653 --> 18:22.833
finally running at full capacity.

18:23.913 --> 18:26.713
But here’s what I realized looking back.

18:26.983 --> 18:29.263
I’d tried everything
before that turning point.

18:29.653 --> 18:34.433
Coffee and energy drinks got me through
the morning, but by two PM, I was done.

18:35.123 --> 18:38.833
Productivity systems lasted a week
before I stopped following them.

18:39.263 --> 18:43.723
Every book gave me three hundred pages
of tips and zero pages of explanation

18:43.723 --> 18:46.943
for why I had no energy for the
first place-- in the first place.

18:47.903 --> 18:52.303
Every app tracked something but never
told me what to actually change.

18:52.783 --> 18:56.203
Every supplement was the answer
until I needed the next one.

18:56.893 --> 18:58.443
I was the perfect customer.

18:58.883 --> 19:03.673
I bought everything, followed every
step, and nothing worked, not because I

19:03.673 --> 19:08.273
lacked discipline, but because none of
it was designed to fix the real problem.

19:08.713 --> 19:12.873
None of it even tried to explain
it, the fundamentals, the

19:12.873 --> 19:14.633
root cause underneath it all.

19:15.163 --> 19:21.773
They sold me Band-Aids when I needed a
surgery. So three years, lost

19:21.773 --> 19:27.723
career, $70,000 gone on decisions
made by brain running on fumes.

19:28.243 --> 19:31.413
That’s what it cost me to find
what the right specialist could

19:31.423 --> 19:33.243
have told me in one session.

19:33.973 --> 19:38.173
I took everything I learned from the
research, from my own trial and error,

19:38.373 --> 19:42.503
from months of testing on myself,
and built it into one protocol.

19:42.693 --> 19:43.843
Dead simple.

19:44.343 --> 19:47.103
I could have done it in a
month if someone had shown me.

19:47.683 --> 19:51.413
I started sharing it with friends,
then their friends reached out.

19:52.383 --> 19:56.593
Every, everyone had the same reaction,
"Why didn’t anyone tell me this before?"

19:57.123 --> 19:59.243
For you, it doesn’t have
to take three years.

20:00.133 --> 20:02.773
Here’s what I want you to see.

20:03.173 --> 20:07.293
You’ve been fixing the symptoms:
productivity apps, focus hacks,

20:07.293 --> 20:10.353
morning routines, but none
of that touches the cause.

20:10.913 --> 20:12.363
Maybe you even went further.

20:12.623 --> 20:13.833
Blood work, normal.

20:14.163 --> 20:15.553
Thyroid panel, normal.

20:15.843 --> 20:19.493
Vitamins D, B12, iron, all fine.

20:19.733 --> 20:20.433
Great news.

20:20.693 --> 20:21.983
That explains nothing.

20:22.563 --> 20:24.853
You’re healthy on paper
and still dragging.

20:25.503 --> 20:28.983
They’re, they are aim-aimed
at the wrong layer.

20:29.413 --> 20:31.123
I know because I tried all of them.

20:31.383 --> 20:33.503
I tried building the
perfect morning routine.

20:33.763 --> 20:37.763
I watched Mark Wahlberg talking
about his four AM workout club.

20:38.053 --> 20:43.123
The guy went to bed at eight PM and
woke up at four AM, and then YouTube

20:43.123 --> 20:45.303
exploded with people trying to copy that.

20:45.633 --> 20:49.633
Like waking up at 4:00 AM
is the secret to success.

20:50.463 --> 20:51.633
I tried Pomodoro.

20:51.633 --> 20:56.503
I tried Notion setups, task manager,
to-do list apps, spent more time

20:56.503 --> 21:00.033
organizing my productivity system
than actually being productive.

21:00.593 --> 21:04.473
I got sucked into the whole
productivity porn rabbit hole.

21:05.063 --> 21:10.563
Cold plunges at six AM, supplements,
magnesium, probiotics, that green powder

21:10.573 --> 21:13.103
subscription every podcast swears by.

21:13.493 --> 21:15.293
Whatever the next guru was selling.

21:16.113 --> 21:19.623
Journaling every morning because
that’s what high-performers do.

21:20.353 --> 21:25.013
Cutting gluten, cutting dairy,
keto, fasting windows, months of

21:25.013 --> 21:28.093
food experiments, because maybe
the fog comes from your gut.

21:28.813 --> 21:32.613
Some types of it makes sense on paper,
but none of it moved the needle.

21:32.723 --> 21:35.873
Not for good, not for me,
not for anyone I know.

21:36.663 --> 21:41.303
And right now I’m building this entire
project: research, script, slides,

21:41.363 --> 21:43.353
everything, without any of that.

21:43.663 --> 21:47.883
No Pomodoro, no fancy task manager,
no supplements, just a brain that

21:47.893 --> 21:49.763
actually works when I sit down.

21:50.133 --> 21:52.993
Because the problem was
never the, never the tools.

21:53.303 --> 21:55.663
The problem was what
was running underneath.

21:56.183 --> 22:01.989
And what runs underneath is sleep. All right,
step back for a second.

22:02.319 --> 22:06.359
Your focus runs on your energy,
and studies show that nothing kills

22:06.359 --> 22:08.259
your energy faster than bad sleep.

22:08.619 --> 22:12.059
Not junk food, not skipping the gym, not even
stress.

22:12.459 --> 22:16.299
When your brain doesn't recover at night,
everything falls apart the next day.

22:16.859 --> 22:19.599
So you're not lazy,
you're not burning out.

22:20.269 --> 22:24.239
You've just been trying to fix the top
of the chain while the bottom was broken.

22:25.459 --> 22:29.979
This is what I've been missing for three
years, and it comes down to three systems.

22:30.189 --> 22:32.369
When they work together, everything works.

22:32.609 --> 22:34.649
When they don't, nothing does.

22:35.269 --> 22:37.799
So let me show you exactly how it works.

22:39.913 --> 22:44.393
Remember when I said you’ve been making
decisions a little drunk every single day?

22:44.743 --> 22:46.473
I wasn’t exaggerating.

22:46.893 --> 22:49.763
Here’s the research:
seventeen hours awake.

22:50.243 --> 22:51.673
That’s not an all-nighter.

22:51.773 --> 22:53.023
That’s just a long day.

22:53.543 --> 22:57.633
Wake up at seven, stay up until midnight,
and at that point, your brain performs

22:57.633 --> 22:59.873
like you had two glasses of wine.

23:00.783 --> 23:04.293
Push it to twenty-four hours,
and you’re at zero point one

23:04.293 --> 23:06.553
zero, roughly a bottle of wine.

23:07.233 --> 23:09.543
Past the legal driving
limit in every state.

23:09.973 --> 23:13.033
And, and no, that’s not stumbling drunk.

23:13.283 --> 23:16.363
At that level, you can
still walk, talk, drive.

23:16.783 --> 23:18.153
People do it every morning.

23:18.543 --> 23:21.063
That’s exactly what
makes it so easy to miss.

23:21.353 --> 23:26.523
The damage sits in the quiet functions:
reaction speed, judgment, focus.

23:26.973 --> 23:32.901
Reaction alone, down by up to half.
Measurable on a test. Invisible

23:32.901 --> 23:36.611
from the inside, and you don't
need an all-nighter to get there.

23:36.931 --> 23:40.041
Weeks of short nights land your brain in the
same zone.

23:40.431 --> 23:42.091
Researchers measure that too.

23:43.081 --> 23:47.381
That's why you fog--
that's what your fog is.

23:47.941 --> 23:52.681
Not a character flaw, not weak
discipline, a brain running week

23:52.701 --> 23:54.601
after week without full recovery.

23:54.791 --> 23:57.431
It doesn't feel drunk, it measures drunk.

23:58.181 --> 24:01.071
And I'm not the only one
who figured this out.

24:01.531 --> 24:05.581
Look at this, real people,
entrepreneurs, developers, founders,

24:05.741 --> 24:10.611
sharing the exact same experience,
same pattern, and same realization.

24:11.411 --> 24:14.041
This isn't a th- this isn't a theory.

24:14.561 --> 24:17.521
This is what happens when people
actually fix the root cause.

24:18.111 --> 24:19.611
And this is just a few.

24:19.881 --> 24:21.611
There are dozens more like this.

24:22.881 --> 24:24.861
And it's not just people on forums.

24:25.231 --> 24:27.561
Look at who else figured this out.

24:28.121 --> 24:32.631
Naval Ravikant, one of the most
respected investors in Silicon Valley.

24:33.021 --> 24:36.521
A clear mind leads to better
judgment, leads to better outcome.

24:36.741 --> 24:39.431
A tired, foggy mind make emotional ones.

24:39.821 --> 24:44.871
LeBron James, four-time championship,
sleeps eight to ten hours,

24:45.191 --> 24:47.001
plus a nap before every game.

24:47.781 --> 24:50.871
He says sleep is the most
important thing for recovery.

24:51.571 --> 24:55.391
Jason Fried built Basecamp into
one of the most profit- profitable

24:55.391 --> 24:57.171
software companies in the world.

24:57.871 --> 25:01.931
It's not worth trading sleep for
a few extra hours at the office.

25:02.391 --> 25:06.571
Not only it will make you exhausted,
it will literally make you stupid.

25:07.031 --> 25:12.051
And Alex Becker, the founder of Hyros,
one of the biggest ad tracking platforms.

25:12.721 --> 25:16.991
Remember I mentioned his course earlier,
and you know what week one was about?

25:17.481 --> 25:22.121
Not ads, not funnels, not
copywriting, sleep and body.

25:23.481 --> 25:27.441
Think about it this way, every single
thing you've experienced with your

25:27.441 --> 25:29.661
sleep comes down to one of these three.

25:30.121 --> 25:35.021
You work until two AM, wake up at seven
for a call, and then wonder why you can't

25:35.021 --> 25:36.891
fall asleep at midnight the next day.

25:37.441 --> 25:38.411
That's the clock.

25:38.631 --> 25:41.401
Your body lost track of when night is.

25:42.231 --> 25:46.311
You're on your third cup of coffee
by noon, and it still doesn't work.

25:46.991 --> 25:49.311
By four PM, your brain just shuts down.

25:49.801 --> 25:50.901
That's the battery.

25:51.311 --> 25:55.451
You keep blocking the signal that tells
your body it needs rest, and now the

25:55.451 --> 25:57.191
signal doesn't come through anymore.

25:57.901 --> 26:01.731
And the worst one, you finally get
to bed at a decent hour, you're

26:01.731 --> 26:06.641
exhausted, and the second your brain,
your head hits the pillow, you start

26:06.641 --> 26:08.751
solving every problem from the day.

26:09.131 --> 26:10.841
Emails, calls, decisions.

26:10.981 --> 26:12.721
You just lie there for hours.

26:13.481 --> 26:14.671
That's the off switch.

26:15.001 --> 26:19.041
Your brain learned that bed is
where you work, not where you rest.

26:20.407 --> 26:24.507
And here's what the research
actually found, and this is

26:24.767 --> 26:26.407
the part nobody tells you.

26:26.867 --> 26:28.777
A good night isn't one thing.

26:29.147 --> 26:31.347
It runs on exactly three conditions.

26:31.767 --> 26:36.297
Your body has to expect sleep at that hour,
that's timing.

26:37.087 --> 26:38.857
It has to actually need sleep.

26:38.957 --> 26:39.877
That's pressure.

26:40.237 --> 26:42.667
And your brain has to be able to let go.

26:42.937 --> 26:43.967
That's the switch.

26:44.657 --> 26:48.947
Break any of one of these three,
and the night falls apart.

26:49.397 --> 26:52.487
Doesn't matter which one,
and there is no fourth.

26:53.517 --> 26:58.327
Look back at what you just saw, the 2:00
A.M. nights, the third coffee that stopped

26:58.337 --> 27:00.757
working, the racing brain on the pillow.

27:01.027 --> 27:07.047
Every one of them is one of these three
breaking, and it cuts the other way too.

27:07.227 --> 27:11.057
The people you just saw, LeBron,
the founders, they're running

27:11.057 --> 27:12.567
on the same three conditions.

27:12.867 --> 27:14.577
Some of them figured it out.

27:14.917 --> 27:16.097
You just heard them.

27:16.417 --> 27:21.267
The rest stumbled into habits that
happened to serve all three without

27:21.437 --> 27:23.307
ever seeing the system underneath.

27:24.447 --> 27:26.627
That's not my framework.

27:26.937 --> 27:28.337
That's how sleep is built.

27:28.607 --> 27:30.967
Science has known this for decades.

27:30.967 --> 27:34.167
It just never made it into
the productivity blogs.

27:34.637 --> 27:38.537
So later, when I show you the
protocol, keep this in mind.

27:38.987 --> 27:41.767
It's built to serve exactly these three.

27:42.157 --> 27:44.387
I didn't pick the number, biology did.

27:45.137 --> 27:47.557
So what are these three systems?

27:49.067 --> 27:52.167
The clock, the battery,
and the off switch.

27:53.067 --> 27:55.567
The clock controls when
your body wants to sleep.

27:55.827 --> 27:59.277
It runs on twenty-four-hour
cycle driven by light.

27:59.857 --> 28:03.037
When it drifts, irregular schedules, no
morning sunlight,

28:03.197 --> 28:05.007
you feel tired at wrong times.

28:05.647 --> 28:08.397
Then the battery is your sleep pressure.

28:08.557 --> 28:10.057
It builds while you're awake.

28:10.387 --> 28:11.897
Caffeine blocks the signal.

28:11.997 --> 28:13.147
It hides the need.

28:13.637 --> 28:16.617
Too much time in bed
without sleeping dilutes it.

28:17.927 --> 28:21.627
And the off switch, that's your
nervous system ability to shift

28:21.627 --> 28:23.837
from go mode to sleep mode.

28:24.547 --> 28:28.177
When it's stuck, racing brain,
3:00 a.m. waking, stress, et

28:28.177 --> 28:30.047
cetera, you can't switch off.

28:31.027 --> 28:34.657
Now, let me take one of three up close.

28:35.197 --> 28:39.287
Let me show you what clock drift
actually looks like in your week.

28:40.537 --> 28:43.627
Ever tried to get real work
done after a long flight?

28:44.007 --> 28:47.837
That feeling where you're staring at
your screen, but nothing's happening.

28:48.307 --> 28:50.537
You read the same email three times.

28:51.027 --> 28:54.337
Someone asks you a simple
question, and your brain just lags.

28:54.867 --> 28:58.487
You're technically awake, but it's
like you're running on autopilot.

28:58.887 --> 29:00.667
Everything takes twice as long.

29:01.107 --> 29:02.267
That's jet lag.

29:03.267 --> 29:05.747
There is a word for the version
most people don't realize

29:05.747 --> 29:07.807
they have: social jet lag.

29:08.347 --> 29:10.467
Same thing, but you didn't fly anywhere.

29:10.587 --> 29:15.087
You go to bed at different times and
wake up at different times, so your body

29:15.127 --> 29:17.107
reacts as if you crossed time zones.

29:17.657 --> 29:19.267
That's where the word comes from.

29:19.507 --> 29:22.747
For most people, it's weekdays versus
weekends.

29:23.027 --> 29:25.737
But if you work from home, it's every single
day.

29:26.227 --> 29:30.627
Monday, you wake up at ten, Wednesday at
noon, Thursday at nine, so your body never

29:30.627 --> 29:35.767
knows what time zone it's in, and you're
trying to do focused work in that state.

29:37.421 --> 29:40.521
And when all three are in sync,
you fall asleep fast, sleep

29:40.521 --> 29:42.271
deep, wake up with energy.

29:42.461 --> 29:47.171
When they're out of sync, you're
getting enough hours, but zero rest.

29:47.911 --> 29:49.131
Sleep isn't a timer.

29:49.141 --> 29:51.381
It's work your brain
has to finish overnight.

29:52.171 --> 29:54.581
Out of sync, that work doesn't get done.

29:55.061 --> 29:57.701
Exhausted but wired, brain fog all day.

29:58.231 --> 30:02.781
This is why you can sleep seven or eight
hours and still feel terrible, still

30:02.781 --> 30:08.021
foggy by noon, still dragging through your
to-do list, still wondering why everyone

30:08.021 --> 30:10.091
else seemed to have more energy than you.

30:11.161 --> 30:12.691
The hours aren't the problem.

30:12.881 --> 30:13.951
The systems are.

30:15.571 --> 30:17.601
This is why nothing you tried worked.

30:18.051 --> 30:19.911
Think of it like baking a cake.

30:20.851 --> 30:24.301
You have all the right ingredients,
but you forgot the main one and

30:24.301 --> 30:25.981
mixed the rest in the wrong order.

30:26.341 --> 30:27.051
No cake.

30:27.861 --> 30:30.551
Exercise without proper
sleep doesn't build you up.

30:31.031 --> 30:32.921
Training is, is the damage.

30:33.421 --> 30:37.881
The, the rebuilding happens at
night, and research links poor

30:37.881 --> 30:42.901
sleep to weaker cellular repair
and faster biological aging.

30:43.261 --> 30:48.471
Your body never gets the repair cycle. And
right ingredient, but no cake.

30:49.381 --> 30:53.281
Eating healthy, but when you sleep
poorly, your hunger hormone spikes,

30:53.461 --> 30:55.311
and it becomes easier to gain weight.

30:55.601 --> 30:58.831
So if you've been blaming food
for the fog, you were close.

30:59.301 --> 31:02.811
But the dial that sets those
hormones is the night before.

31:03.351 --> 31:09.241
On top of that, remember, your prefrontal
cortex isn't running at full power either,

31:09.581 --> 31:11.911
so controlling what you eat gets harder.

31:12.251 --> 31:14.591
Right ingredient and again, no cake.

31:15.741 --> 31:21.101
You process tons of information every day:
calls, decisions, research, conversations.

31:21.281 --> 31:24.761
But without proper sleep, your
brain can't sort through any of it.

31:25.341 --> 31:27.531
You work harder but think slower.

31:28.111 --> 31:30.071
Right ingredient and no cake.

31:30.491 --> 31:35.281
Everything was aimed at one piece,
maybe two, never the foundation.

31:36.241 --> 31:37.641
And here's the honest part.

31:38.261 --> 31:40.571
None of those tools is garbage.

31:40.761 --> 31:43.451
But what the tool gives you
was never about the tool.

31:44.161 --> 31:45.751
It's about what it lands on.

31:46.401 --> 31:49.051
Your morning routine, if
you have one, that's a clock

31:49.051 --> 31:50.791
adjustment, partial at best.

31:51.301 --> 31:53.551
Melatonin, great tool, wrong job.

31:53.961 --> 31:57.691
It's a timing signal for one
system, not a sleep switch.

31:58.431 --> 32:02.551
Take it without knowing your,
timing, and you're guessing.

32:03.421 --> 32:04.961
Turn off your phone before bed.

32:05.311 --> 32:09.431
One small piece of off switch while
two other systems stay broken.

32:10.001 --> 32:15.271
Pomodoro, focus apps, time blocking, those
live from a different layer entirely.

32:15.921 --> 32:18.571
They manage the symptoms of foggy brain.

32:18.791 --> 32:20.931
They don't touch what's producing it.

32:21.571 --> 32:26.481
Real levers, most of them, each one
pulling on one system out of three.

32:26.851 --> 32:27.391
Blind.

